# SOC Triage Agent

`constitution.md` is binding. These rules restate the non-negotiables for every change.

## Synthetic data only
Use fixture alerts and public sample data. Never ingest real customer, employer, or classmate PII. Do not add live SIEM, EDR, or ticket connectors in the MVP.

## No offensive tooling
Detection and triage only. Do not add exploit code, payload generation, credential theft, scanning, or attack rehearsal.

## Human in the loop
Recommend severity and draft notes. A human makes the final call. Never auto-close, auto-block, or auto-notify external parties.

## Explainability and safe defaults
Every triage decision must cite alert fields and rule reasons. If data is missing or confidence is low, mark `needs_review` instead of guessing critical. Severity, confidence, needs_review, and reasons come from the rules engine. Optional LLM text may rewrite the note only. On the CLI it runs solely when `--llm-notes` is set. On the local API it runs only when the request sets `llm_notes=true` or the server was started with `--llm-notes`. Either path also needs `SOC_TRIAGE_LLM_PROVIDER=openai_compatible` with a base URL and model. An API key is sent only if `SOC_TRIAGE_LLM_API_KEY` is set. Missing config, timeout, or invalid text keeps the template note.

## Secrets stay out of git
Do not commit API keys or `.env`. `.env.example` may list variable names with empty values. The rules-only path must run with no secrets and no network call. Gate the LLM client behind an explicit opt-in (`--llm-notes` on the CLI, `llm_notes=true` or `serve --llm-notes` on the local API) plus provider settings. Skip the call when that config is absent. A local Ollama server may omit the API key. The local API binds `127.0.0.1` by default, has no authentication, and is a demo until auth is added.

## Honest scope
Do not describe this repo as a production SIEM. Match README claims to what the code actually does. Implement one `tasks.md` item at a time. Keep `constitution.md`, `spec.md`, `plan.md`, and `tasks.md` current when behavior changes.
